NAME
       ls - list directory contents

SYNOPSIS
       ls [OPTION]... [FILE]...

DESCRIPTION
       List information about the FILEs (the current directory by default).
       Entries are sorted alphabetically, ignoring leading dots and case. On a
       terminal the output is arranged in columns that fit the window and names
       are colored by type: directories blue, executables green, images
       magenta, archives red, source files yellow.

OPTIONS
       -a, --all
           do not ignore entries starting with ., including . and ..

       -A, --almost-all
           like -a, but without . and ..

       -l
           long listing: permissions, links, owner, group, size, modification
           time and name

       -h, --human-readable
           with -l, print sizes like 1.5K, 23M, 2.1G

       -1
           one entry per line

       -m
           comma separated list

       -R, --recursive
           list subdirectories recursively

       -d, --directory
           list directories themselves, not their contents

       -t
           sort by modification time, newest first

       -S
           sort by file size, largest first

       -X
           sort by extension

       -U
           do not sort; list in directory order

       -r, --reverse
           reverse the sort order

       -F, --classify
           append indicator: / directory, * executable, @ link, | fifo, =
           socket

       -i, --inode
           print the inode number of each file

       -n
           like -l, but list numeric user and group IDs

       -o
           like -l, but do not list the group

       --group-directories-first
           list directories before files

       --color[=WHEN]
           colorize names: always, auto (default) or never

EXAMPLES
       ls -lh /bin
       ls -la ~
       ls -lt --color=never | head
       ls -R /usr/share

SEE ALSO
       tree(1), stat(1), find(1), du(1)
